ent":"`detail[\"reason\"]` of `compute_unavailable` tells `out_of_memory` from `no_device`. Job workers' process exit codes follow their own convention (`codes.EXIT_*`): `EXIT_RETRY = 75` asks to be run again, `EXIT_CANCELLED = 130` means cancelled.","locale":"en"},"10397":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"page","content":"Conventions","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions","locale":"en"},"10398":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions","content":"The conventions code in Mizuoto follows: style, the layer rules, the rules for native code, the exactness rule, the steps for adding a command, route, setting or engine option, what tests a change needs, documentation in both languages and the commit message format.","locale":"en"},"10399":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"Code style","locale":"en"},"10400":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"The layer rules","locale":"en"},"10401":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"Rules for native code","locale":"en"},"10402":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"Exactness","locale":"en"},"10403":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"Adding things","locale":"en"},"10404":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#tests-a-change-needs","content":"Tests a change needs","locale":"en"},"10405":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#documentation","content":"Documentation","locale":"en"},"10406":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#commit-messages","content":"Commit messages","locale":"en"},"10407":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions","content":"This page lists the conventions a change to Mizuoto must follow. Most of them are held by tests or checks: `python scripts/dev.py check` passing is the least a change needs before it is merged. The design behind each convention is in Architecture and Design references and decisions.","locale":"en"},"10408":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Python ≥ 3.10**, with 3.10–3.13 supported. `ty` checks types for 3.10, so do not use syntax or standard-library modules newer than that (`tomllib` needs its `tomli` fallback).","locale":"en"},"10409":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**ruff and ty are unpinned**, and `pyproject.toml` writes `lint.select` out rule by rule (`E4`, `E7`, `E9`, `F`, `I`, `UP`, `B`, `SIM`, `RUF100`, `ANN`), so a ruff upgrade cannot quietly change the rule set. Line length 180, indent 4. ty runs with `error-on-warning`. `python scripts/dev.py format` formats and applies the safe fixes; `lint` and `typecheck-py` only check.","locale":"en"},"10410":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Every function is annotated** (under `mizuoto/` and `packages/native/`; tests and scripts are exempt). `Any` only where the value really is anything (JSON).","locale":"en"},"10411":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Every model that crosses the API boundary is a `Record`** (`mizuoto/core/record.py`, pydantic v2, `json_schema_serialization_defaults_required`): settings, requests, responses, events, `--json` output. Defaulted fields are required in the output schema, so the generated TypeScript types match what the server sends.","locale":"en"},"10412":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Heavy imports go inside functions**: av and numpy hot paths in the core, fastapi and uvicorn in the command line, and every piece of model code in `core/inference`. That keeps `mizuoto version` and `mizuoto --help` fast, and tests check it.","locale":"en"},"10413":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**No method named `list` on a class that annotates with `list[...]`** (ty resolves the name to the method). Write `list_models`, `list_voices`, `list_presets`.","locale":"en"},"10414":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Identifiers and times** come from `mizuoto/core/clock.py`: `new_id(prefix)` (a hex millisecond timestamp and 8 random hex digits, sorting roughly by creation time) and `now_iso()` (UTC, milliseconds, ending in `Z`, sorting as text).","locale":"en"},"10415":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Errors are the classes of `mizuoto/core/errors.py`**, and error codes live only in `mizuoto/core/codes.py`. A fix for the user goes in `detail[\"hint\"]`: the command line prints it as a second line and the UI shows it under the error. Routes and commands never make up their own HTTP statuses or exit codes.","locale":"en"},"10416":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"**Logging** goes through the `mizuoto` logger (`logging.getLogger(__name__)` under `mizuoto.*`); only the entry point (`setup_logging` in `mizuoto/logger.py`) configures handlers.","locale":"en"},"10417":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#code-style","content":"Code that follows an upstream library line for line and must stay comparable with it (`mizuoto/nn/dsp/kaldi.py`, torchaudio's fbank) is kept verbatim and exempted from lint rules in `pyproject.toml`, not rewritten.","locale":"en"},"10418":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"`tests/test_architecture.py` checks every module's imports with `ast`; the tables are in Architecture. In short:","locale":"en"},"10419":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"`core`, `api` and `cli` never import torch, torchaudio, transformers, onnxruntime or a licence-gated engine package, not even inside a function. `core` reaches `runtime`, `nn`, `engines` and `training` only inside functions of `core/inference`.","locale":"en"},"10420":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"Routes and commands are thin: parse the input, call **one** core method, shape the output. Each operation has one core method, so the API's JSON has the shape of the command line's `--json`. Business logic never goes into `api/` or `cli/`.","locale":"en"},"10421":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"Engines import only `runtime`, `nn`, `text`, `training`, `core.codes`, `core.inference.tasks` and their own package, and **never another engine**. Code two engines share goes into `nn` or `runtime`.","locale":"en"},"10422":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"`core/codes.py`, `core/jobs/events.py`, `core/jobs/jsonl.py`, `core/remote/frames.py` and `core/inference/tasks.py` use the standard library only: training stages, remote frames and engines all use them.","locale":"en"},"10423":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#the-layer-rules","content":"A new third-party dependency goes into `pyproject.toml` and into its layer's allowed list in `tests/test_architecture.py`, with the reason it is needed (see Release).","locale":"en"},"10424":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"Engines, trainers, the runtime and the server share one process, so code under `runtime`, `nn`, `text`, `engines` and `packages/native` keeps these rules (details in Runtime):","locale":"en"},"10425":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Change no process-wide state.** Do not seed the global generators, change `os.environ`, the working directory or `sys.path`, call `logging.basicConfig`, `torch.set_default_dtype`, `torch.use_deterministic_algorithms` or `torch.set_float32_matmul_precision`, assign `torch.backends.*` or `torch._inductor.config`, or use `sdpa_kernel` (it switches SDPA's kernels for every thread). `compute.fast`'s switches are set only in `mizuoto/runtime/accel/features.py`, called by the host and the training stage.","locale":"en"},"10426":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Randomness comes from the request.** Use `ctx.generator_for(device)` and `ctx.rng`; pass the generator to every random op (`torch.randn(..., generator=g)`), and draw in upstream's order, shapes and device. Calls that take no generator, such as `randn_like`, are rewritten in an equivalent explicit form. Where upstream draws from the global generator while it builds a model, replay the draws on a private generator.","locale":"en"},"10427":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Loading draws nothing.** Modules are built from `runtime.ops` layers (`ops.default` skips random initialisation), and checkpoints load strictly (a missing or unexpected key is an error unless the engine lists it as ignorable).","locale":"en"},"10428":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Load outside inference mode, and leave parameters as `nn.Module` makes them** (no `requires_grad_(False)`). Two CPU kernel traps change results: on a non-contiguous 3-D input, `F.linear` takes another kernel when the weight `requires_grad`, or when it is an inference tensor (made inside `torch.inference_mode()`), even under `no_grad` or inference mode. Upstream's weights are ordinary parameters that require grad, so native engines keep them so; requests still run in inference mode.","locale":"en"},"10429":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Attention goes through `accel.attention(q, k, v, site=…)`**, never SDPA or its aten kernels directly, so `compute.attention` can switch the backend (see Attention acceleration).","locale":"en"},"10430":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Download nothing.** Every file an engine reads is a model file or a shared asset, which `ModelService` downloads and hands over through `LoadSpec`.","locale":"en"},"10431":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Key a module-level cache by its whole input** (every parameter, dtype and device), or build per call. A global cache keyed by part of its input makes a second configuration in the same process reuse the first one's result.","locale":"en"},"10432":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Place components per phase and estimate memory per request.** Engines put each phase's components on the device with `ctx.use` / `ctx.run`; a module whose weights are read outside its `forward` is marked `fixed`; a module that needs float64 is placed through `DeviceService.float64_device`. See Memory management.","locale":"en"},"10433":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#rules-for-native-code","content":"**Be cancellable.** Call `ctx.check_cancel()` in every loop (autoregressive token, ODE step, segment, beam step). `Cancelled` derives from `BaseException`; never swallow it with a broad `except BaseException`.","locale":"en"},"10434":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"Each Mizuoto engine is a port of an upstream project and must give **the same** results: on the CPU in fp32 with the same seed, the audio is identical sample for sample (for trainers: losses, learning rates and saved weights identical byte for byte). This rule decides much of how code is written:","locale":"en"},"10435":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"**Reproduce first, fix after.** Upstream's quirks (draw order, padding, alignment edge cases) are reproduced as they are and shown identical with the parity harness; a fix, where one is needed, is a separate change with the deviation and its reason recorded.","locale":"en"},"10436":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"**Record every deviation.** Each place a port differs from upstream (to run on every platform, to leave global state alone, because upstream depended on thread timing…) goes into the engine's notes and the design record `AGENTS.md`, with the comparison's numbers.","locale":"en"},"10437":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"**Speed work keeps results.** Performance work (CUDA graphs, removed waits, compilation, weight streaming) must give the same tokens and the same audio; each round is compared with the previous one by `scripts/parity/perf.py` and `train_perf.py` (see Performance work). An optimisation that does change bits (Qwen3-TTS's `fused_projections`, `compute.kv_cache = static`) can only be an opt-in setting, and is listed in the output's parameters.","locale":"en"},"10438":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#exactness","content":"On GPUs in reduced precision, bit identity is not required; modules are judged by their error and whole runs statistically. The tiers and tolerances are in Testing.","locale":"en"},"10439":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"To add","locale":"en"},"10440":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"Steps","locale":"en"},"10441":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A command","locale":"en"},"10442":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A plain function in `mizuoto/cli/commands/` with `--json`, registered in `get_app()` of `cli/app.py` (name and help at registration), and added to `EXPECTED_TREE` in `tests/cli/test_cli.py`. See Adding a command","locale":"en"},"10443":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A route","locale":"en"},"10444":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"The core method (`Record` in and out) → a route with an `operation_id` in `api/routers/` → `python scripts/dev.py typegen` and commit `schema.d.ts` → `tests/api/test_api.py`. See Adding a route","locale":"en"},"10445":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"An event","locale":"en"},"10446":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"An `EventBase` subclass (`__event_name__`) in `core/events/models.py`; it enters the OpenAPI schema by itself. Regenerate the types and handle it in the UI's `api/socket.ts`","locale":"en"},"10447":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A setting","locale":"en"},"10448":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A field with a default and a docstring in its group in `core/settings/models.py`; when the UI shows it, a control in Settings, the text in both languages and `typegen`; document it in both language versions of Configuration. A change to `engines.<id>.*` unloads that engine's models, which load with the new settings next time","locale":"en"},"10449":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"An engine option","locale":"en"},"10450":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"`[options.<name>]` (or a load option, `[load_options.<name>]`) in the manifest, and the same entry in the engine's `OPTION_SCHEMA`; a test requires the two to match. See Adding an engine","locale":"en"},"10451":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A job kind","locale":"en"},"10452":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"`jobs.register(kind, factory)` in the service's constructor, the factory returning a `JobSpec` with its resources (`engine:<engine>:<device>`, `gpu:<device>`, `cpu`, `io`…); the lists it changes when it ends are registered in `AFFECTS` in the UI's `socket.ts`","locale":"en"},"10453":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"An engine or a trainer","locale":"en"},"10454":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"See Adding an engine and Trainers","locale":"en"},"10455":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"A screen","locale":"en"},"10456":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#adding-things","content":"See Web UI: components only from `@/ui`, styles only from tokens, text in both languages at once","locale":"en"},"10457":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#tests-a-change-needs","content":"**Every change comes with tests**, and `python scripts/dev.py check` (lint, typecheck-py, test-py, test-web, typegen-check) passes. CI runs the same tests on Linux, Windows and macOS with Python 3.10–3.13, so mind how paths, encodings and line endings behave on Windows.","locale":"en"},"10458":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#tests-a-change-needs","content":"The core's behaviour is tested with the dummy engine (no torch, no model); the network is replaced by `httpx.MockTransport`; tests get an isolated data folder and an empty environment from `make_services`.","locale":"en"},"10459":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#tests-a-change-needs","content":"**A change to an engine or trainer**: update its tiny-model tests in `tests/engines_native/` (including the streaming check on a simulated device), run the contract suite with real models, and compare with upstream through the parity harness.","locale":"en"},"10460":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#tests-a-change-needs","content":"When the API changes, regenerate the types and commit them, or `typegen-check` fails. UI changes pass the rule tests (imports, tokens, layout, layers, i18n keys).","locale":"en"},"10461":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#documentation","content":"**Both languages.** Every page of this site has `<slug>.mdx` (Simplified Chinese, the default) and `<slug>.en.mdx` (English), section for section; the sidebars are `meta.json` and `meta.en.json`, in the same order. A test checks that every Chinese page has its English twin. The README comes in two (`README.md`, `README.zh-CN.md`), and the UI's text lives in `en.ts` and `zh-CN.ts`, whose keys must match.","locale":"en"},"10462":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#documentation","content":"**A change in behaviour changes the documentation**: what users see goes into the guide, command-line or advanced pages, what developers need into the development pages.","locale":"en"},"10463":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#documentation","content":"**The design record.** `AGENTS.md` at the repository root records what the code does, the rules it keeps and why, and durable findings (such as which upstream behaviours a port reproduces to give the same output). Update it with any change of design or new finding.","locale":"en"},"10464":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#documentation","content":"Chinese pages keep the existing pages' terms (音色, 合成, 克隆, 模型, 引擎, 共享资源, 许可, 任务…). Write what is and why, not how it came about.","locale":"en"},"10465":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#commit-messages","content":"Commit messages take the form `type(scope): summary`, the summary in lower case, saying what the change does:","locale":"en"},"10466":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#commit-messages","content":"Types: `feat` (a feature), `fix`, `perf` (faster with the same results), `refactor` (no change in behaviour), `test`, `docs`, `build` (builds, CI, Docker), `chore`. A breaking change adds `!` after the type, as in `refactor!:`.","locale":"en"},"10467":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#commit-messages","content":"The scope is the part affected: `runtime`, `webui`, `training`, `models`, `scripts`, `site`, or an engine id (`qwen3tts`, `gptsovits`…); several may be listed (`runtime,training`), or none for a change across the board.","locale":"en"},"10468":{"id":"/en/docs/development-conventions-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-conventions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-conventions#commit-messages","content":"The body says why, the measured or compared results, and any behaviour deliberately kept.","locale":"en"},"10469":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"page","content":"Design references and decisions","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design","locale":"en"},"10470":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"Where Mizuoto's design comes from: the skeleton's lineage, what was taken from InvokeAI, ani2xcur, Material Design 3 and ComfyUI and what was not, and a table of design decisions with their reasons.","locale":"en"},"10471":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Lineage","locale":"en"},"10472":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"From InvokeAI: FastAPI plus a single-page app","locale":"en"},"10473":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"Shipping the front end inside the wheel","locale":"en"},"10474":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"Port selection","locale":"en"},"10475":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"The command line: Typer registration","locale":"en"},"10476":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#material-design-3-in-layers","content":"Material Design 3 in layers","locale":"en"},"10477":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"From ComfyUI: the runtime","locale":"en"},"10478":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#design-decisions","content":"Design decisions","locale":"en"},"10479":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Environment and distribution","locale":"en"},"10480":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Engines and exactness","locale":"en"},"10481":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Runtime and memory","locale":"en"},"10482":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Jobs, interfaces and data","locale":"en"},"10483":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"Mizuoto was not designed from nothing. Its application skeleton (core services, thin API and command line, a Vue UI shipped in the wheel, the tooling) comes from a line of sibling projects, and its model runtime follows ComfyUI. This page records what was taken from each source and why, what was deliberately left out, and finally the design decisions that shaped the code as it is. What the code looks like is in Architecture.","locale":"en"},"10484":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Project","locale":"en"},"10485":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"What it is","locale":"en"},"10486":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"What Mizuoto keeps of it","locale":"en"},"10487":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"SD Model Hub","locale":"en"},"10488":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"A Stable Diffusion model manager","locale":"en"},"10489":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"The skeleton itself: all logic in `core` with two thin entrances over it (FastAPI and Typer), `build_services()` assembling the services, the `Record` base, domain errors carrying an HTTP status and an exit code, three settings layers (host overrides > environment > file), the event bus and the socket bridge, the security middleware, port binding and `server.json`, `embed.py` for embedding, `scripts/dev.py`, and the layer rule held by a test","locale":"en"},"10490":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Hanaikada","locale":"en"},"10491":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"An AI image browser on the same skeleton","locale":"en"},"10492":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Proof that the skeleton carries over to another domain; its image metadata and index have nothing to do with Mizuoto","locale":"en"},"10493":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"rvc-next","locale":"en"},"10494":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"An RVC voice-conversion application on the same skeleton","locale":"en"},"10495":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"The skeleton under machine-learning load: a job queue with named resources and priorities, persisted requests and retry, job workers with JSON-lines events, memory holders and release after running out of memory, a voice library, training experiments with stage fingerprints; the Material 3 front end (`theme/`, `ui/`, motion, layers, shell, stores, i18n, rule tests); the documentation site","locale":"en"},"10496":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Mizuoto 0.1","locale":"en"},"10497":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"One Python environment and one worker process per engine, upstream code vendored as it was","locale":"en"},"10498":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Capabilities declared per variant, two-tier parameters (common parameters plus engine options), licence gates, the voice library and model catalogue, the parameter mappings; and the behaviour the native engines are compared with (git tag `v1-engine-workers`)","locale":"en"},"10499":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"Mizuoto 0.2","locale":"en"},"10500":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"One environment, every engine implemented in-tree on a ComfyUI-style runtime","locale":"en"},"10501":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"The code as it is","locale":"en"},"10502":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lineage","content":"The skeleton is GPL-3.0, and so is Mizuoto (`GPL-3.0-only`). The two licence-gated engines ship apart because of their licences (see the decisions below).","locale":"en"},"10503":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"InvokeAI's front-end/back-end structure is where the skeleton started. What was taken:","locale":"en"},"10504":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**One OpenAPI schema as the contract.** Every route has an explicit `operation_id`, with pydantic models in and out. `mizuoto/api/openapi.py` adds every event model and a `ServerEvents` map (event name → schema) to the schema, so socket payloads are typed like REST. `scripts/dev.py typegen` generates `schema.d.ts` with `openapi-typescript` and it is committed; `typegen-check` catches drift between the API and the types in CI.","locale":"en"},"10505":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**Commands go over REST; the socket only pushes.** socket.io sits at `/ws`, server to client only. REST is the truth, and events only patch or invalidate the client's query cache; a reconnect refetches, so missed events never leave the UI stale.","locale":"en"},"10506":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**Long work is a job plus events**, so requests return quickly, and progress and cancellation come with it.","locale":"en"},"10507":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**Blocking work stays off the event loop.** Routes that touch the disk or the database are plain `def` (they run in the thread pool); events from job threads cross to the loop with `call_soon_threadsafe`.","locale":"en"},"10508":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**Thin routes over a service layer**, one router module per resource on the server and one query module per resource in the UI.","locale":"en"},"10509":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"**Relative URLs plus a development proxy**: front-end code uses the same relative URLs in development and production.","locale":"en"},"10510":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"UI preferences are stored on the server through `/api/v1/client-state/{key}`, so they follow the user across browsers.","locale":"en"},"10511":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Where Mizuoto differs on purpose:","locale":"en"},"10512":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"InvokeAI","locale":"en"},"10513":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Mizuoto","locale":"en"},"10514":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Why","locale":"en"},"10515":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"A static service locator, `ApiDependencies.invoker`","locale":"en"},"10516":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"`app.state.services` plus the FastAPI dependency `ServicesDep`","locale":"en"},"10517":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"A route's dependencies are in its signature and tests can pass isolated services; no graph nodes need to share services","locale":"en"},"10518":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Multi-user: JWTs, sliding refresh, socket rooms, per-user redaction","locale":"en"},"10519":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"One optional access token (a Bearer header, the `mizuoto_token` cookie or `?token=`), plus a Host check (against DNS rebinding) and an Origin check on state-changing requests; binding to a non-loopback address logs a warning","locale":"en"},"10520":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"A single user or a small trusted group running it locally; the complexity pays only for multi-tenant serving","locale":"en"},"10521":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Parsing `/openapi.json` at run time into node templates","locale":"en"},"10522":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Types generated at build time only","locale":"en"},"10523":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"No back-end types are extended by users","locale":"en"},"10524":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"React, Redux Toolkit, RTK Query","locale":"en"},"10525":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"Vue 3, Pinia, TanStack Vue Query, `openapi-fetch`","locale":"en"},"10526":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-invokeai-fastapi-plus-a-single-page-app","content":"The skeleton's existing front end","locale":"en"},"10527":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"`mizuoto/webui/` is both the Vue project and a Python package. Vite builds the UI into `mizuoto/webui/dist/`; `dist/` is not committed and goes into the wheel as package data, so after `pip install` users need neither Node nor bun.","locale":"en"},"10528":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"The server finds `dist/` through the package's `__path__` (`web_dist_dir()` in `api/static.py`): one line for a source checkout, an installed wheel and a container. `SPAStaticFiles` is mounted last, so it only receives paths the API, the socket and the docs did not match. Without a build it logs a warning and serves the API only, which is the normal state while working on the back end.","locale":"en"},"10529":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"Unknown paths fall back to `index.html` only for requests that accept `text/html` (client-side routes survive reloads and deep links), so a mistyped asset or API path still gets a 404. Content-hashed files under `assets/` are cached for a year (`immutable`); everything else, `index.html` included, is `no-cache`, so after an upgrade a browser never pairs an old UI with a new back end.","locale":"en"},"10530":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"The build uses `base: './'`, and at run time `api/baseUrl.ts` derives the deployment root from the entry script's URL (everything before the last `/assets/`); routes use hash history. One build works at the root and under an `--api-prefix` sub-path with no rewriting.","locale":"en"},"10531":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#shipping-the-front-end-inside-the-wheel","content":"The UI is built before the wheel: `scripts/build_wheels.py` runs `bun install --frozen-lockfile` and `bun run build`, then builds the sdist and the wheel; Docker builds it in a bun stage of its own, and the final image carries no Node toolchain. See Release.","locale":"en"},"10532":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"The server decides whether a port is free by **binding** it, not by connecting to it (`bind_first_free_port` in `mizuoto/core/net/ports.py`):","locale":"en"},"10533":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"It creates a listening socket and binds it to the configured host and port; on `EADDRINUSE` or `EACCES` (which covers Windows' excluded port ranges) it tries the next port, up to 20 attempts and never past 65535, and fails with a clear error when none is free. `SO_EXCLUSIVEADDRUSE` on Windows, `SO_REUSEADDR` elsewhere.","locale":"en"},"10534":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"The bound socket is handed to uvicorn (`server.run(sockets=[sock])`): there is no gap between checking and serving.","locale":"en"},"10535":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"Strict mode (`server.strict_port`, `--strict-port`) fails instead of moving on, for reverse proxies and containers with fixed port mappings.","locale":"en"},"10536":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#port-selection","content":"Other programs learn the actual port: a line `SERVER_READY url=…` on standard output, and `server.json` (host, port, URL, process id) in the data folder, removed on shutdown. `mizuoto runtime` and friends find the running server through it. The front end derives the back end's address from the URL it was loaded from and needs no notice.","locale":"en"},"10537":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"The command line follows ani2xcur's Typer layout:","locale":"en"},"10538":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"`get_app()` in `mizuoto/cli/app.py` registers every command and group in one function, with names and help given at registration; that file is the command line's table of contents. Command functions (`cli/commands/*.py`) are plain, undecorated functions.","locale":"en"},"10539":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"The app is built inside a function, not at import time, so each test gets a fresh one; `EXPECTED_TREE` in `tests/cli/test_cli.py` compares the whole command tree, so a forgotten registration fails.","locale":"en"},"10540":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"`typer_factory()` (`cli/factory.py`) gives every app and group the same settings; `DebugOptionTyper`, `DebugTyperCommand` and `AlphabeticalMixedGroup` make `--debug` work at every level and sort the help. The imports of Typer's private `typer._click` live only in that file, and `typer` has an upper bound (`<0.28`).","locale":"en"},"10541":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"`main()` handles errors itself: a `MizuotoError` prints its message and `detail[\"hint\"]` and exits with its exit code, Ctrl+C exits 130, anything else prints a traceback and exits 1.","locale":"en"},"10542":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#the-command-line-typer-registration","content":"Heavy dependencies are imported inside command functions, so `mizuoto version` loads neither FastAPI nor torch. `python -m mizuoto` equals `mizuoto`.","locale":"en"},"10543":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#material-design-3-in-layers","content":"The UI uses Material Design 3 in two layers:","locale":"en"},"10544":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#material-design-3-in-layers","content":"**The token layer** (`mizuoto/webui/src/theme/`): colour roles `--md-sys-color-*` are generated by `@material/material-color-utilities`' Tonal Spot scheme from one seed colour (by default `#4f6b3a`), light and dark, at three contrast levels; type, shape, motion and state layers are CSS variables under Google's official names. The UI uses tokens only, never literal colours.","locale":"en"},"10545":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#material-design-3-in-layers","content":"**The component layer** (`src/ui/`): the components `@material/web` has (buttons, icon buttons, FAB, checkbox, filter chip, divider, text field, slider, progress, switch, tabs) are wrapped in project Vue components; the rest (shell, navigation, dialogs, sheets, menus, cards, snackbar, stepper, charts, waveforms…) are built from tokens. Feature code imports only from `@/ui`.","locale":"en"},"10546":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#material-design-3-in-layers","content":"`@material/web` is in maintenance mode and its component set no longer grows; the wrapper layer keeps that risk inside `src/ui/`, where a component that breaks is replaced without touching feature code. MUI is not used (it implements Material Design 2), nor is M3 Expressive. Icons are Lucide (imported only in `ui/icons.ts`), and the Roboto Flex font ships with the package rather than loading from outside. Details in Web UI.","locale":"en"},"10547":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"The model runtime (`mizuoto/runtime/`) follows ComfyUI. ComfyUI is a source of patterns, not a code base adopted: Mizuoto keeps its own services, jobs, API, command line and UI, and code comments name the ComfyUI counterpart of each piece.","locale":"en"},"10548":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"From ComfyUI","locale":"en"},"10549":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"In Mizuoto","locale":"en"},"10550":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"Device and memory management (`model_management.py`): device probes, free memory (driver free plus torch's cached-but-unused), a memory reserve, hardware precision tables, `load_models_gpu` in most-recently-used order, partial loading and dynamic VRAM, unloading every idle model after running out of memory, a pinned-memory budget","locale":"en"},"10551":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`runtime/device.py` (`DeviceService`), `runtime/manager.py` (`ModelManager`), `runtime/policy.py`, `runtime/stream.py`, all service objects rather than module globals. See Memory management","locale":"en"},"10552":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"The model wrapper (`ModelPatcher`)","locale":"en"},"10553":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`ModelHandle` in `runtime/handle.py`, without the diffusion parts: whole or partial placement, weights streamed unit by unit, host copies","locale":"en"},"10554":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`ops` injection (`comfy.ops`)","locale":"en"},"10555":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`runtime/ops.py`: `ops.default` skips random initialisation (fast construction, no random draws while loading), `ops.cast` casts weights to the input's dtype and device on every forward","locale":"en"},"10556":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"Attention selection (one signature, SDPA by default, optional backends imported under guard with fallbacks)","locale":"en"},"10557":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`runtime/accel/`: every attention site goes through `accel.attention`, the backend chosen by `compute.attention`, with self-tests, constraint checks and fallbacks. See Attention acceleration","locale":"en"},"10558":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"Loading and detection (safetensors or `weights_only` pickles, key renaming)","locale":"en"},"10559":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`runtime/loading.py`, strict by default; detection looks at the catalogue's explicit metadata first (sizes and SHA-256), then at each engine's files-only `detect_variant`","locale":"en"},"10560":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"In-tree language-model code (Qwen/Llama families without transformers, seeded generators, per-token interrupts)","locale":"en"},"10561":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`runtime/lm/` (the Qwen2/3 decoder with transformers' numerics), `runtime/sampling.py`, `runtime/graphs.py`","locale":"en"},"10562":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"Audio DSP ported (no torchaudio)","locale":"en"},"10563":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"`mizuoto/nn/dsp/`, bit-exact with torchaudio, librosa and Whisper","locale":"en"},"10564":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"What was not taken:","locale":"en"},"10565":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"**Module-level mutable globals** (the VRAM state, the loaded-models list, the interrupt flag, command-line arguments read deep in the code). Mizuoto has `Services` and settings objects, and passes the device and cancel token explicitly, because a CPU job and a GPU job can run at once.","locale":"en"},"10566":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"**Giant files and long `if` chains** (such as model detection matched in order). Each engine has its own detection, configuration and construction in its own package.","locale":"en"},"10567":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"**The node graph and the custom-node ecosystem.** A typed engine registry with entry points is enough, and the licence-gated engines register exactly that way.","locale":"en"},"10568":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"The diffusion machinery (samplers, hooks, ControlNet), and running language models as text encoders.","locale":"en"},"10569":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"**Dependencies on `comfy-aimdo` (a native VRAM allocator) and an exactly pinned `comfy-kitchen`.** Memory management is built on torch's own allocator.","locale":"en"},"10570":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#from-comfyui-the-runtime","content":"**Deliberate numerical divergence from reference implementations** (such as a fused RMSNorm by default). Mizuoto keeps the reference maths on the default path; fused variants that change bits are opt-in.","locale":"en"},"10571":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#design-decisions","content":"The decisions that shaped the code as it is: what was decided, and why.","locale":"en"},"10572":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"10573":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Reason","locale":"en"},"10574":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"One Python environment, every engine ported in-tree and run in the server's own process; no upstream code vendored","locale":"en"},"10575":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"The engines' dependency conflicts (transformers 4.51 to 5.3, torch 2.3 and 2.8, numpy 1 and 2) all came from upstream code using library internals and from upstream pins, not from what the models need. Ported, they need one torch and one set of dependencies, and the engine environments, worker processes and inter-process protocol go","locale":"en"},"10576":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"The cost of running in process (a crash in native code takes the server down; no memory release by process exit) is paid for by rules","locale":"en"},"10577":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Native code changes no global state (tested), requests are checked for residue, unloading really frees memory; an engine can run on a remote agent where process isolation is wanted; training stays in child processes","locale":"en"},"10578":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Fish Speech and IndexTTS are distributions of their own (`mizuoto-fishspeech`, `mizuoto-indextts`), installed into the same environment and registered under the entry-point group `mizuoto.engines`","locale":"en"},"10579":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Their licences (Fish Audio Research License, bilibili Model Use License) cover the code; a port is a derivative, and folding it into the GPL-3.0 `mizuoto` would conflict","locale":"en"},"10580":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Each distribution has its own version and is checked and released on its own; a gated package names the Mizuoto versions it works with (`mizuoto>=0.2.0,<0.3`)","locale":"en"},"10581":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"A fix to one engine should not wait for, or force, a release of the others. The range is tested at both ends: on Mizuoto at each commit, and on the oldest release it allows","locale":"en"},"10582":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"torch is a dependency, but its build (CUDA, ROCm, XPU, MPS, CPU) is installed by the user first; `mizuoto doctor` prints the command for the machine","locale":"en"},"10583":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"The right build depends on the hardware and driver, which no package manager can choose for the user","locale":"en"},"10584":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"No transformers, onnxruntime, torchaudio or librosa at run time","locale":"en"},"10585":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"They bring version conflicts, platform limits or global state; tokenizers come from `tokenizers`, `tiktoken` and `sentencepiece`, and ONNX models' weights are converted into torch modules by an in-tree protobuf reader","locale":"en"},"10586":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Optional extras only where a platform limit requires one: `gptsovits-ja` (`pyopenjtalk==0.4.1`, source only), `gptsovits-ko`, `mizuoto-indextts[ja]`","locale":"en"},"10587":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Every required dependency has wheels on every platform CI covers; without an extra, text in that language fails with `capability_not_supported` and the install command","locale":"en"},"10588":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Japanese through `pyopenjtalk==0.4.1`, not pyopenjtalk-plus; Simplified → Traditional through the C++ `opencc==1.4.2`; Chinese segmentation through `jieba` instead of `jieba_fast`","locale":"en"},"10589":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"pyopenjtalk-plus changes OpenJTalk's rules (18 % of Japanese sentences differ) and the pure-Python OpenCC gives other results; `jieba` has `jieba_fast`'s dictionary and results, in pure Python","locale":"en"},"10590":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"The optional attention packages (flash-attn, xFormers, SageAttention) are backends only, with version checks, self-tests and fallbacks, never dependencies","locale":"en"},"10591":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"They support only some platforms; the default path must work everywhere with unchanged results","locale":"en"},"10592":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"One Docker image holds the server, the UI and every engine; the licence-gated engines come with `GATED=1`","locale":"en"},"10593":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#environment-and-distribution","content":"Users choose only torch's build; the licences are still accepted at run time","locale":"en"},"10594":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"10595":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Reason","locale":"en"},"10596":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Each port matches its reference sample for sample (CPU, fp32, same seed); the reference is how Mizuoto 0.1 actually called upstream, not upstream's demos","locale":"en"},"10597":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"A user's output should not change because the implementation did; where 0.1 differed from upstream on purpose (F5-TTS generating chunks in order rather than on a thread pool), 0.1 is the reference","locale":"en"},"10598":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Upstream's quirks are reproduced first and fixes land as separate changes; even quirks of no product value are reproduced (IndexTTS-1.5 using no KV cache below fp16)","locale":"en"},"10599":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Mixing fixes with porting makes a parity failure ambiguous; identical behaviour is the default, and a deviation needs a reason and a record","locale":"en"},"10600":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Trainers reproduce upstream's recipes, including their training/inference mismatches (Qwen3-TTS's training skips `text_projection`; Fish Speech's training does not scale the codebook embeddings by 1/√11)","locale":"en"},"10601":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Losses, learning rates and exported weights then match upstream byte for byte, and the exports load in both implementations","locale":"en"},"10602":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"CosyVoice is compared with its reference running on torch 2.8","locale":"en"},"10603":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"On its own torch 2.3.1 and on 2.8, sampling already diverges","locale":"en"},"10604":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Speech recognition is a native Whisper large-v3-turbo reproducing 0.1's pipeline (overlapping 30 s windows, 5-beam search, the seek loop inside each window, the merge)","locale":"en"},"10605":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Identical transcripts, without CTranslate2's and cuDNN's version pairing","locale":"en"},"10606":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"The Whisper encoder lives in the ASR engine and is not shared with CosyVoice's speech tokenizers","locale":"en"},"10607":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"s3tokenizer's attention (its scaling, its masks) is another computation","locale":"en"},"10608":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"CosyVoice's and IndexTTS's CAM++ are not merged into one shared asset; nor is the Qwen emotion model in IndexTTS 2's and 2.5's folders","locale":"en"},"10609":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Merging would make CosyVoice need a gated engine's licence; the emotion model lives inside each model's repository folder, and sharing it would mean moving installed models' files","locale":"en"},"10610":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"The catalogue downloads only files the engines read","locale":"en"},"10611":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"E.g. CosyVoice-BlankEN's weights and the semantic codec IndexTTS-2.5 never reads only took space","locale":"en"},"10612":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"GPT-SoVITS runs the plain T2S with its steps replayed as CUDA graphs, not upstream's CUDA-graph T2S","locale":"en"},"10613":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Upstream's accelerated path changes results; replaying the eager kernels does not","locale":"en"},"10614":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"VoxCPM's text normalisation uses wetext 0.1.2's own FSTs, shipped with the package","locale":"en"},"10615":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Upstream pins wetext 0.1.2, whose FSTs differ from both CosyVoice's and IndexTTS's","locale":"en"},"10616":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"VoxCPM's reference is upstream's own package","locale":"en"},"10617":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Mizuoto 0.1 never had VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"10618":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Capabilities are static (Qwen3-TTS 0.6B CustomVoice is its own variant `custom_voice_lite`, without `instruct`)","locale":"en"},"10619":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"Capabilities must be known before a model loads, for planning and the UI","locale":"en"},"10620":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"OmniVoice designs voices from attributes only","locale":"en"},"10621":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"The model takes a closed attribute vocabulary","locale":"en"},"10622":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"No ZipEnhancer denoising, timestamps or CUDA `optimize` for VoxCPM; no Fish Speech S2 Pro int8 in the catalogue","locale":"en"},"10623":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"The former need another model and a ModelScope pipeline, and are all off in upstream's default generation; there is no public int8 repository","locale":"en"},"10624":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"F5-TTS's licence is recorded as `cc-by-nc-4.0`, not gated, with tagged outputs","locale":"en"},"10625":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#engines-and-exactness","content":"That is the weights' licence; the non-commercial term is conveyed by tags and a notice","locale":"en"},"10626":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"10627":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Reason","locale":"en"},"10628":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-158","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"No estimate refuses a load: what does not fit loads partly and streams, at the minimum if need be; only an allocation that really fails is an error, after which every GPU's idle models are unloaded","locale":"en"},"10629":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-159","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"As ComfyUI does; estimates come from file sizes, and refusing would block requests that fit","locale":"en"},"10630":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-160","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Weights stream through forward hooks on units and layers that swap `param.data`, not through code in the `ops` layers","locale":"en"},"10631":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-161","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"It covers every code path (`layer.forward_step`, `vae.decode`, custom layers), and costs nothing while a model is whole on its device","locale":"en"},"10632":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-162","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Only linear and convolution weights stream, never embeddings","locale":"en"},"10633":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-163","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Codebook embeddings are often read through `.weight` directly (cosine searches, `F.embedding`); ComfyUI likewise streams only its ops layers","locale":"en"},"10634":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-164","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"GPT-SoVITS's T2S stays whole on its device","locale":"en"},"10635":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-165","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Its blocks run through `prompt` / `step`, which read the fused projections directly; the model is 150 MB","locale":"en"},"10636":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-166","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Tiled decoding as an out-of-memory fallback only for some decoders (ASR, GPT-SoVITS, F5-TTS's Vocos, IndexTTS 2's BigVGAN, VoxCPM's VAE)","locale":"en"},"10637":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Qwen3-TTS's codec already decodes in chunks; CosyVoice's HiFT carries a phase across the whole utterance that tiles would break; the rest are short","locale":"en"},"10638":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"When a training run's estimated memory exceeds the free memory, the log warns with the preset that would fit; the run is not refused","locale":"en"},"10639":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"The estimate comes from file sizes and is not calibrated on a GPU","locale":"en"},"10640":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"The training memory tools offer what keeps results and a few things that change them slightly; no Adafactor, no splitting micro-batches on out-of-memory","locale":"en"},"10641":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Those change the recipes beyond what the presets promise","locale":"en"},"10642":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"`compute.max_resident_models` caps resident models across engines (default 3), the least recently used idle model going first","locale":"en"},"10643":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-173","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Every engine's models share one process and its memory","locale":"en"},"10644":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-174","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"The host probes devices without importing torch: nvidia-smi, rocm-smi / amd-smi and the platform first, a child process only when torch's build could see a GPU the tools missed","locale":"en"},"10645":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-175","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Listing devices or serving the UI should not pay a torch import of a second or two","locale":"en"},"10646":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-176","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"CUDA graphs are on by default; `torch.compile` is off by default","locale":"en"},"10647":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-177","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"CUDA graphs replay the same kernels with the same results; compiling changes floating-point results and costs the first request its compilation","locale":"en"},"10648":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-178","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Attention's `causal` is SDPA's `is_causal` (top-left); bottom-right causality is expressed through `MaskInfo`; dropout draws from torch's default generator","locale":"en"},"10649":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-179","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Every site passes `is_causal` as upstream did, and CosyVoice's streamed-text steps rely on the top-left form; the only site with dropout (GPT-SoVITS's T2S training) calls SDPA's own","locale":"en"},"10650":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-180","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"The attention policy is bound per request step for inference, and process-wide for a training stage","locale":"en"},"10651":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-181","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"Autograd runs backward passes, and checkpointed recomputations, on threads of its own","locale":"en"},"10652":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-182","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"External attention kernels are called directly, not wrapped as Mizuoto's own `torch.library` custom ops","locale":"en"},"10653":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-183","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#runtime-and-memory","content":"flash-attn and xFormers register their own operators and backward passes; SageAttention has no backward and is never used under autograd","locale":"en"},"10654":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-184","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Decision","locale":"en"},"10655":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-185","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Reason","locale":"en"},"10656":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-186","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Named resources with capacities (`gpu:<device>`, `engine:<engine>:<device>`, `inference`, `cpu`, `io`, `project:<id>`) rather than one global GPU lock","locale":"en"},"10657":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-187","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Serialising per engine and per device covers inference and training alike, and work on different devices runs in parallel","locale":"en"},"10658":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-188","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A training run is one `train` job whose steps are its stages, each stage a child process; the stages are declared statically in the manifest","locale":"en"},"10659":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-189","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The run holds the device once; the UI and the fingerprints know the stages without running anything","locale":"en"},"10660":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-190","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Any output line of a job worker resets its watchdog, and a training stage also beats every 10 s","locale":"en"},"10661":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-191","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A stage that logs is evidently alive; a silent one still beats","locale":"en"},"10662":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-192","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Only requests that differ in nothing but their text, with one segment and no seed, join a micro-batch","locale":"en"},"10663":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-193","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A batch draws its random numbers differently; voices and parameters are per call","locale":"en"},"10664":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-194","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A project's lines render as ordinary speak jobs; takes are outputs of kind `line`, stale by request fingerprint","locale":"en"},"10665":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-195","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"One synthesis path serves every feature, so micro-batching and every engine come for free","locale":"en"},"10666":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-196","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A remote agent owns its models and engines; requests carry files by content (SHA-256); training stays local","locale":"en"},"10667":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-197","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A path on one machine means nothing on another","locale":"en"},"10668":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-198","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The model mirror (`mizuoto-model`) is a source in front of the official ones, laid out as Mizuoto keeps files","locale":"en"},"10669":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-199","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A partial mirror works: what it lacks comes from the official repositories file by file, checked against the same SHA-256","locale":"en"},"10670":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-200","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Accepting a licence is also required to download the weights and assets; the full text ships with the package and the command line can show it","locale":"en"},"10671":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-201","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The licences cover the weights; a reader must have the text in front of them before accepting","locale":"en"},"10672":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-202","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Voices, models, datasets, experiments and projects are folders with JSON; listing is a scan; the database holds only what is queried","locale":"en"},"10673":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-203","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The folders are the truth, and a scan cannot drift from the files; hundreds of voices scan in milliseconds","locale":"en"},"10674":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-204","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Engines get references as `cache/refs/<sha256>.wav` (16-bit WAV); voices store FLAC","locale":"en"},"10675":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-205","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Every engine reads 16-bit WAV; registering by content avoids duplicates","locale":"en"},"10676":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-206","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A missing transcript is looked up in the cache while planning, else transcribed in the job; without ASR, `clone_no_transcript` or an error with a hint","locale":"en"},"10677":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-207","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Planning should not wait on a model, but the result must be usable","locale":"en"},"10678":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-208","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"`mizuoto doctor [engine]` checks in one place the torch build against the hardware, what each engine misses, extras and 0.1's old environments","locale":"en"},"10679":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-209","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Everything between the machine and the engines is said in one place","locale":"en"},"10680":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-210","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"`mizuoto runtime` asks the running server (`server.json`) first, and reports its own process only when there is none","locale":"en"},"10681":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-211","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A command-line process has no models loaded; the server does","locale":"en"},"10682":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-212","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Reading server paths, revealing files and the like work only for a browser on the server's own machine","locale":"en"},"10683":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-213","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A tunnel's or proxy's visitors must not browse the server's disk","locale":"en"},"10684":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-214","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Streamed audio's binary messages are bare PCM16 and everything else is JSON text; the browser player has no buffer cap","locale":"en"},"10685":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-215","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A binary message is always audio; when the model falls behind the player waits instead of dropping speech","locale":"en"},"10686":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-216","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The OpenAPI schema is generated without `separate_input_output_schemas=False`","locale":"en"},"10687":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-217","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"With it, every defaulted response field would become optional in the generated types; the split it avoids only touches `SpeakRequest`","locale":"en"},"10688":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-218","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"The error codes `download_failed` (exit 12) and `install_failed` (13)","locale":"en"},"10689":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-219","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Each needs its own hint and exit code","locale":"en"},"10690":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-220","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"A name cannot be both a command and a group: `mizuoto env vars`; parameter presets are `--use-preset/-P`, since `--preset` is the preset speaker","locale":"en"},"10691":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-221","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#jobs-interfaces-and-data","content":"Typer's limit; `--preset` was taken by speakers first","locale":"en"},"10692":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"page","content":"Adding an engine","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine","locale":"en"},"10693":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"How an engine is written and wired in, with OmniVoice as the example: the manifest and its files manifest, the Engine and LoadedEngine contract, model handles, task types, errors, assets, registration, and the tests that hold it to upstream.","locale":"en"},"10694":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"1\\. The manifest","locale":"en"},"10695":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#variants-and-capabilities","content":"Variants and capabilities","locale":"en"},"10696":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Parameters, options and load options","locale":"en"},"10697":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#models-and-files-manifests","content":"Models and files manifests","locale":"en"},"10698":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"Assets, licence and training","locale":"en"},"10699":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recognising-model-folders","content":"Recognising model folders","locale":"en"},"10700":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#2-the-implementation","content":"2\\. The implementation","locale":"en"},"10701":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`Engine`","locale":"en"},"10702":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`LoadedEngine`","locale":"en"},"10703":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"Task types","locale":"en"},"10704":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Model handles","locale":"en"},"10705":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#errors","content":"Errors","locale":"en"},"10706":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"3\\. Registration","locale":"en"},"10707":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"4\\. Tests","locale":"en"},"10708":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"OmniVoice, the worked example","locale":"en"},"10709":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"Checklist: what a new engine touches","locale":"en"},"10710":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"An engine in Mizuoto is two things in two places, plus its tests:","locale":"en"},"10711":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"Part","locale":"en"},"10712":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"Where","locale":"en"},"10713":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"Read by","locale":"en"},"10714":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The manifest: variants, capabilities, parameters, options, catalogue models, assets, licence, training methods","locale":"en"},"10715":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"`mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/<id>.toml`, files manifests in `manifests/files/`","locale":"en"},"10716":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The core (`EngineRegistry`), without importing the engine","locale":"en"},"10717":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The implementation: an `Engine` subclass and its `LoadedEngine`, built on `mizuoto.runtime`, `mizuoto.nn` and `mizuoto.text`","locale":"en"},"10718":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"`mizuoto/engines/<id>/`, or `packages/native/<id>/` for a distribution of its own","locale":"en"},"10719":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The runtime, in the server's process","locale":"en"},"10720":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"Tests","locale":"en"},"10721":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"`tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py`, the contract suite, `scripts/parity/`","locale":"en"},"10722":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"CI, and you","locale":"en"},"10723":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine","content":"The core plans and validates every request from the manifest alone, then calls the implementation through `core/inference` (see Runtime). This page walks through OmniVoice: `mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/omnivoice.toml` and `mizuoto/engines/omnivoice/`.","locale":"en"},"10724":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/*.toml` ships in the `mizuoto` package; `EngineRegistry` loads every file in the folder and validates it as `EngineManifest` (`mizuoto/core/engines/models.py`). The manifest of a licence-gated engine ships in `mizuoto` too, so the catalogue and the licence text are there before its distribution is installed. The top level:","locale":"en"},"10725":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Field","locale":"en"},"10726":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"10727":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`id`","locale":"en"},"10728":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"The engine id; the implementation is registered under the same id","locale":"en"},"10729":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`devices`","locale":"en"},"10730":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Device kinds the engine runs on (`cuda`, `cpu`, `mps`, `xpu`)","locale":"en"},"10731":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`languages`","locale":"en"},"10732":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Canonical language codes; `*` means any","locale":"en"},"10733":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`[upstream]`","locale":"en"},"10734":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"The project and commit the port reproduces (documentation, not a dependency)","locale":"en"},"10735":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`ops`, `internal`","locale":"en"},"10736":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`call` operation names; a helper engine (ASR) not offered for synthesis","locale":"en"},"10737":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"`[weights]`","locale":"en"},"10738":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#1-the-manifest","content":"Roles and suffixes of a voice's own fine-tuned weights (GPT-SoVITS: `gpt = \".ckpt\"`, `sovits = \".pth\"`)","locale":"en"},"10739":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#variants-and-capabilities","content":"A variant is a set of static capabilities; requests are validated against it before any job exists:","locale":"en"},"10740":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#variants-and-capabilities","content":"The full list is `Capability` in `models.py`. Some drive the pipeline: without `long_text` Mizuoto segments the text; `batch` with `max_batch > 1` enables micro-batching; `stream_audio`'s granularity comes from `stream_granularity`; `stream_text_input` allows streamed text over the WebSocket. A variant may also have `languages`, `speakers`, `emotions`, `inline_tags`, `instruct_requires` and `weights_version`.","locale":"en"},"10741":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Table","locale":"en"},"10742":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Model","locale":"en"},"10743":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"10744":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[params.<common name>]`","locale":"en"},"10745":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`ParamSpec`","locale":"en"},"10746":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Only names from `CommonParam` (`temperature`, `top_p`, `num_steps`, `speed`, `seed`…). `target` is the engine's own name; `default` only shows the engine's default, Mizuoto never imposes it; `approximate = true` adds a warning to results; `variants` and `streaming` narrow where it applies","locale":"en"},"10747":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[options.<name>]`","locale":"en"},"10748":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"10749":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Engine-specific options (`engine_options`); unknown ones always raise; `group = \"hidden\"` keeps one out of forms. The engine's `OPTION_SCHEMA` must mirror them exactly","locale":"en"},"10750":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`[load_options.<name>]`","locale":"en"},"10751":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"`OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"10752":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#parameters-options-and-load-options","content":"Applied when a model loads (`engines.<id>.load_options`, the engine card's Options), such as F5-TTS's `ode_method` or IndexTTS's `use_torch_compile`. They reach the engine in `LoadSpec.options`","locale":"en"},"10753":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#models-and-files-manifests","content":"`files` (`ModelFiles`) may also have `subdir`, `into`, `include`, `exclude` and `unpack`; `extra` lists more repositories; `assets` names shared assets; `vram_mb` is the catalogue's memory figure shown with the model (loading never refuses on it).","locale":"en"},"10754":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#models-and-files-manifests","content":"After adding or changing a model, run `python scripts/dev.py model-manifests omnivoice`. `scripts/model_manifests.py` pins the Hugging Face revision to the current commit and lists every file with its size and SHA-256 (LFS files from their metadata, small files fetched and hashed) into `manifests/files/<manifest>`. Downloads verify against it; `test_catalogue_models_have_files_manifests` checks that every catalogue model has one.","locale":"en"},"10755":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[[assets]]`**: files several models of an engine share (GPT-SoVITS's encoders and text front-end data, IndexTTS-2's w2v-BERT…), described like models, installed in `<models>/_assets/<engine>/<name>/`. A model names the ones it needs in `assets`; the engine gets their folders as `spec.options[\"assets\"]` (asset id → folder).","locale":"en"},"10756":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[licence]`**: with `gated = true`, downloading the weights and assets and synthesis need `engines.<id>.accepted_licence == id@version`; `file` names the full text in `manifests/licences/`; `trains_others = false` refuses the outputs as training data for other engines; `notice` shows under results; `commercial` tags outputs.","locale":"en"},"10757":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#assets-licence-and-training","content":"**`[training.<id>]`**: training methods, run by the engine's `TRAINER`; see Trainers.","locale":"en"},"10758":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#recognising-model-folders","content":"`mizuoto model import` identifies the engine and variant from file contents alone: `mizuoto/core/models/detect.py` reads configuration files (OmniVoice: `model_type == \"omnivoice\"` in `config.json`), and the engine's `detect_variant` must agree.","locale":"en"},"10759":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#2-the-implementation","content":"An engine is a package under `mizuoto/engines/<id>/` whose `__init__.py` exports the `Engine` subclass, and whose modules import only what the layer rules allow: `mizuoto.runtime`, `mizuoto.nn`, `mizuoto.text`, `mizuoto.training`, `mizuoto.core.codes`, `mizuoto.core.inference.tasks`, its own package, and numpy, torch and the text libraries; never another engine. Its module imports no torch at the top (`test_engine_contract_import_is_light` and `test_manifest_options_match_the_engines` import engine modules without torch): torch is imported inside the methods, and type hints go under `if TYPE_CHECKING:`.","locale":"en"},"10760":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Member","locale":"en"},"10761":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"10762":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`id`","locale":"en"},"10763":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The engine id (the manifest's and the registry's)","locale":"en"},"10764":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`VERSION`","locale":"en"},"10765":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The implementation's own version string (default `\"1\"`). Voice caches are keyed by Mizuoto's version, not this one","locale":"en"},"10766":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`OPTION_SCHEMA`","locale":"en"},"10767":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`OptionSpec`s with exactly the manifest's `[options]`: same type, default, min, max and choices (`test_manifest_options_match_the_engines`)","locale":"en"},"10768":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`TRAINER`","locale":"en"},"10769":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`\"module:Class\"` of the engine's `mizuoto.training.Trainer`, when it trains","locale":"en"},"10770":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`EXTRAS`","locale":"en"},"10771":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Optional features: install spec → the modules it provides, e.g. GPT-SoVITS's `{\"mizuoto[gptsovits-ja]\": (\"pyopenjtalk\",), \"mizuoto[gptsovits-ko]\": (\"mecab\", \"jamo\")}`; `mizuoto doctor` lists the ones that do not import","locale":"en"},"10772":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`detect_variant(model_dir)`","locale":"en"},"10773":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"The variant from the folder's files only, without torch","locale":"en"},"10774":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`load(spec, runtime, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10775":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"Build the model from `spec` (`model_id`, `model_dir`, `variant`, `device`, `dtype`, `options`) and return a `LoadedEngine`; report with `ctx.progress(\"loading:<part>\")`, check `ctx.check_cancel()` between parts","locale":"en"},"10776":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"`call(op, payload, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10777":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#engine","content":"An operation that needs no loaded model","locale":"en"},"10778":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"One loaded model. It is not re-entrant: the job scheduler gives it one request at a time (the `engine:<engine>:<device>` resource).","locale":"en"},"10779":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"10780":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Required","locale":"en"},"10781":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"10782":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`describe()`","locale":"en"},"10783":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"10784":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"A `Description`: sample rate, capabilities, speakers, languages, VRAM, `ignored_params`","locale":"en"},"10785":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`synthesize(task, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10786":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"10787":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"A generator: `yield AudioChunk(samples, sample_rate, segment, final)` as soon as each piece is ready, calling `ctx.check_cancel()` at every step; record the values actually used in `ctx.applied` and warnings with `ctx.warn()`","locale":"en"},"10788":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`prepare_voice(voice, cache_dir, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10789":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Derived voice state (prompt codes, embeddings) written into `cache_dir`, returned as `VoiceCache(files, meta)`; later requests get the files in `VoiceSpec.cache`","locale":"en"},"10790":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`convert(task, ctx)`, `edit(task, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10791":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Voice conversion and speech editing, generators too; by default they raise `Unsupported`","locale":"en"},"10792":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`call(op, payload, ctx)`","locale":"en"},"10793":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Operations named in the manifest's `ops` (ASR's `transcribe`, Fish Speech's `codec_encode` / `codec_decode`)","locale":"en"},"10794":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`memory_required(task)`","locale":"en"},"10795":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Bytes of device memory a request needs beyond the weights; the model manager makes room for it","locale":"en"},"10796":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"`close()`","locale":"en"},"10797":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"Drop the weights (the manager has already taken the handles off their devices)","locale":"en"},"10798":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#loadedengine","content":"An engine returns float32 mono audio in `[-1, 1]` at the model's own rate and never encodes: joining, normalising, resampling and encoding are the core's job. It receives resolved, absolute paths that exist (references are 16-bit WAV in `cache/refs/`) and never downloads. The core has validated the request against the manifest, so an engine maps rather than checks.","locale":"en"},"10799":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"The requests and results are standard-library dataclasses in `mizuoto/core/inference/tasks.py`, re-exported by `mizuoto.runtime.engine`:","locale":"en"},"10800":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"Type","locale":"en"},"10801":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"Fields","locale":"en"},"10802":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`SynthesisTask`","locale":"en"},"10803":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`text`, `language`, `voice`, `style` (`instruct`, `emotion`), `params` (common parameters by Mizuoto's names), `options` (engine options), `texts` (a batch), `segment` / `segments`, `stream`, `text_stream`","locale":"en"},"10804":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`VoiceSpec`","locale":"en"},"10805":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`references` (`Reference`: `audio_path`, `transcript`, `language`, `sample_rate`, `duration`), `preset`, `description`, `attributes`, `weights`, `cache`, `voice_id`","locale":"en"},"10806":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`VoiceConversionTask`, `SpeechEditTask`","locale":"en"},"10807":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"A source clip and a voice; a clip, its transcript and `EditSpan`s","locale":"en"},"10808":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"`LoadSpec`, `Description`, `AudioChunk`, `VoiceCache`, `OptionSpec`","locale":"en"},"10809":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#task-types","content":"What `load` gets, what `describe` returns, the audio, a voice cache, an option","locale":"en"},"10810":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Every module with weights is wrapped in a `ModelHandle` and listed in `self.handles`, so the model manager can place it, offload it and report its size:","locale":"en"},"10811":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Build modules from `ops.default` (no random initialisation), load weights with `mizuoto.runtime.loading` (`load_state_dict`, `load_into`, strict), and leave parameters as `nn.Module` makes them. Draw every random number from `ctx.generator_for(device)` or `ctx.rng`, in upstream's order (see Runtime).","locale":"en"},"10812":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#model-handles","content":"Compute attention with `mizuoto.runtime.accel.attention(q, k, v, site=SITE, …)`, never `F.scaled_dot_product_attention` (an architecture test forbids it): declare `SITE = accel.Site(\"<id>.<part>\")` once per call site, and pass the arguments upstream passed to SDPA (`causal` is its `is_causal`). A site where upstream wrote attention by hand gives that code as its `reference`; one that only its reference may compute is `swappable=False` (attentions with relative positions inside the scores are not sites at all: they stay hand-written). Where the site builds a mask from a structure (padding on one side, causal, a window), pass `mask_info` too, so faster kernels can take the call (Acceleration).","locale":"en"},"10813":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#errors","content":"Raise `EngineError(message, detail, code=codes.…)` with a code from `mizuoto.core.codes` (`invalid_input`, `capability_not_supported`, `model_missing`…); put the fixing command in `detail[\"hint\"]`. `Unsupported(message)` is `capability_not_supported`. The core rebuilds the domain error from the code; running out of memory and other exceptions are mapped for you.","locale":"en"},"10814":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"The runtime finds an implementation by id (`get_engine` in `mizuoto/runtime/engine.py`):","locale":"en"},"10815":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"**In this distribution**: add the engine to `BUILTIN`:","locale":"en"},"10816":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"**In a distribution of its own** (a licence that cannot ship in `mizuoto`, decision D1): register the class under the entry-point group `mizuoto.engines` in its `pyproject.toml`, depend on `mizuoto`, and add the engine id → distribution name to `DISTRIBUTIONS` in `mizuoto/core/inference/service.py`, so a missing install is reported by name. Fish Speech's:","locale":"en"},"10817":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"Entry points are read once per process: installing such a distribution means restarting Mizuoto. Its package follows the engines' rules and mirrors its manifest (`check_native_package`, `check_options_mirror`, called by its `tests/test_<id>_package.py`), has its own version in `mizuoto_<id>/version.py` with the Mizuoto versions it works with in its `pyproject.toml` (`check_gated_distribution`), is checked by CI's `gated` and `oldest-mizuoto` jobs and is built by `scripts/build_wheels.py native` (see Release).","locale":"en"},"10818":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#3-registration","content":"An engine is *available* when torch imports (the dummy engine needs none) and its implementation is registered; otherwise `EngineStatus.missing` names what is missing and the UI and `mizuoto doctor` show the fix.","locale":"en"},"10819":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"`tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py` builds a tiny model of the engine's own architecture with random weights in a temporary folder (config files, a byte-level vocabulary, safetensors written by the test) and drives the real code path on the CPU: loading, voice prompts and their cache, decoding, batches, seeds that repeat, cancellation. No weights are downloaded, so CI runs it on every push (CPU torch). `test_omnivoice_tiny.py` is a complete example; trainers have `test_<id>_train_tiny.py`.","locale":"en"},"10820":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"`tests/engines/test_contract.py` (markers `engine` and `weights`, deselected by default) drives the whole stack (plan → job → runtime → engine) for every installed catalogue model of the available engines: `describe` agrees with the manifest; every declared voice mode is audible, at the right rate, about as long as the text needs, and says the text (word error rate below 0.35 when ASR is installed); a seed repeats; two segments split; cancelling mid-generation stops the engine; and no process-wide state is left behind. A new engine id goes into the suite's `engines` settings override. See Testing.","locale":"en"},"10821":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"Mizuoto's standard for a port is identical output to the upstream implementation on the CPU in fp32 under a seed. For the engines Mizuoto 0.1 had, `scripts/parity/` runs the same cases through 0.1's stack and the native one and compares them sample for sample (see Testing). Record what the port reproduces, where it departs and the parity result on its page in the engine reference (`site/content/docs/engines-<id>.mdx` and `.en.mdx`), and any rule a later change must not break in `AGENTS.md`.","locale":"en"},"10822":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#4-tests","content":"Look at random numbers first: upstream may draw them while building a model or preparing a reference (HuBERT's layer drop, IndexTTS's w2v-BERT, CosyVoice 3's vocoder noise), or with tensors of other strides. Then at tensor shapes and kernels: SDPA with or without a mask, a mask's dtype, contiguity, the CPU thread count. `AGENTS.md` §2b lists such findings engine by engine.","locale":"en"},"10823":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`mizuoto/engines/omnivoice/` (about 2 900 lines in all):","locale":"en"},"10824":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"Module","locale":"en"},"10825":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"What","locale":"en"},"10826":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`engine.py`","locale":"en"},"10827":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`OmniVoiceEngine` and `LoadedOmniVoice`: loading, voice prompts, the iterative decoding, chunked long text, decoding and post-processing","locale":"en"},"10828":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`model.py`","locale":"en"},"10829":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"The diffusion language model on `mizuoto.runtime.lm.qwen`'s decoder","locale":"en"},"10830":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`audio_tokenizer.py`","locale":"en"},"10831":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"The HiggsAudio v2 tokenizer: `nn/codecs/dac`, `nn/ssl/hubert`, residual codebooks","locale":"en"},"10832":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`audio.py`, `text.py`, `duration.py`, `voice_design.py`, `lang_map.py`","locale":"en"},"10833":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"Silence removal and fades as pydub computes them; text chunking and punctuation; the duration estimate; instructions from design attributes; language names","locale":"en"},"10834":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`train.py`","locale":"en"},"10835":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"`OmniVoiceTrainer`: methods `full` and `lora`","locale":"en"},"10836":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"What it shows:","locale":"en"},"10837":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Loading**: `OmniVoiceModel(config, ops.default)` filled by `load_into(model, load_state_dict(folder / \"model.safetensors\"))`, cast to the dtype (bf16 on a GPU that has it, else fp16; fp32 on the CPU; an explicit `engines.omnivoice.dtype` wins); the audio tokenizer the same way; the text tokenizer from `mizuoto.text.tokenize.bpe.load_bpe(folder)`; two handles.","locale":"en"},"10838":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Parameter mapping**: Mizuoto's common names map onto upstream's generation config (`num_steps → num_step`, `temperature → class_temperature`), engine options fill the rest.","locale":"en"},"10839":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Three voice modes**: references → a voice prompt; attributes → an instruction from `instruct_from(attributes)`; neither → the model's own voice. A free-text description raises `capability_not_supported`.","locale":"en"},"10840":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Voice cache**: `prepare_voice` saves the prompt as `prompt.pt`; `synthesize` prefers `voice.cache[\"prompt\"]`.","locale":"en"},"10841":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Randomness**: the Gumbel noise draws from `ctx.generator_for(self.device)`; HuBERT's layer-drop draws (made even in eval mode upstream) from `ctx.generator_for(\"cpu\")`.","locale":"en"},"10842":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Cancel and progress**: `ctx.check_cancel()` at every decoding step, `ctx.progress(\"generating\", …)` after it.","locale":"en"},"10843":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Batches**: with `task.texts` set, every text in one batch, each yielded with `segment = task.segment + index`.","locale":"en"},"10844":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"**Memory**: `memory_required` estimates the logits' working memory per batch row.","locale":"en"},"10845":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#omnivoice-the-worked-example","content":"OmniVoice's engine page records the parity result (identical to 0.1 on the CPU in fp32 for cloning, design, its own voice and the fine-tuned model) and what exactness took.","locale":"en"},"10846":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto/core/engines/manifests/<id>.toml`; run `dev.py model-manifests <id>`; the licence text in `manifests/licences/` when gated.","locale":"en"},"10847":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto/engines/<id>/` (or `packages/native/<id>/`): the `Engine` subclass with `OPTION_SCHEMA` mirroring the manifest, its `LoadedEngine`, and the modules it needs; shared parts into `mizuoto/nn` or `mizuoto/runtime`.","locale":"en"},"10848":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"Registration: `BUILTIN` in `mizuoto/runtime/engine.py`, or the `mizuoto.engines` entry point plus `DISTRIBUTIONS`. `mizuoto/core/models/detect.py` recognises its model folders; optional packages go in an extra of `pyproject.toml` and in `EXTRAS`.","locale":"en"},"10849":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"Tests: `tests/engines_native/test_<id>_tiny.py` (an engine in its own distribution keeps them in its package's `tests/`, as `packages/native/<pkg>/tests/` do); the engine id list in `tests/core/test_engines.py::test_registry_lookups` and the `engines` override in `tests/engines/test_contract.py`.","locale":"en"},"10850":{"id":"/en/docs/development-engine-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-engine","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-engine#checklist-what-a-new-engine-touches","content":"`mizuoto model download <id>/<name>`, then the contract suite on the real models; record the port and its parity on the engine's page (`engines-<id>`, both languages), add it to the sidebars (`meta.json`, `meta.en.json`) and to the engine reference table, and add rules to `AGENTS.md` if there are any.","locale":"en"},"10851":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"page","content":"Memory","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory","locale":"en"},"10852":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"How the runtime spends GPU memory and RAM, after ComfyUI: phases, the budget and VRAM modes, units and weight streaming, host copies, partial loading and eviction, the exact out-of-memory retry, working-memory estimates and their calibration, simulated devices, training presets, and what an engine does to take part.","locale":"en"},"10853":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Where it lives","locale":"en"},"10854":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"Phases","locale":"en"},"10855":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"The budget","locale":"en"},"10856":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Units: what streams","locale":"en"},"10857":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"Weight streaming","locale":"en"},"10858":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"Host copies and RAM","locale":"en"},"10859":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Eviction","locale":"en"},"10860":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Running out of memory","locale":"en"},"10861":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"Working-memory estimates","locale":"en"},"10862":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"Reporting","locale":"en"},"10863":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"Static KV cache and the CUDA allocator","locale":"en"},"10864":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"Simulated devices and tests","locale":"en"},"10865":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Training","locale":"en"},"10866":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"Writing an engine for it","locale":"en"},"10867":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"A model runs whenever its smallest working set fits the device, not only when all of it fits. Mizuoto's runtime does this the way ComfyUI does (`load_models_gpu`, `ModelPatcher`'s partial loading, dynamic VRAM), without a native allocator: a request places only the components its current step needs; when they do not fit, other components give up memory unit by unit; when that is still not enough, the components load partly and the rest of their weights stream from RAM as each layer runs. Running out of memory becomes slower, not failed.","locale":"en"},"10868":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"None of this changes a result. A streamed layer computes with the same tensors as a resident one, a retried step replays the same random draws, and the tests run every engine with its weights streamed and compare the audio with the plain run. The mechanisms that do change numbers (tiled decoding, the static KV cache, the `lowest` training preset) are opt-in and say so in the output.","locale":"en"},"10869":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory","content":"The user-facing settings are in Performance; the runtime as a whole in Runtime.","locale":"en"},"10870":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Part","locale":"en"},"10871":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Code","locale":"en"},"10872":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Role","locale":"en"},"10873":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`VramPolicy`","locale":"en"},"10874":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/policy.py`","locale":"en"},"10875":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"The mode, reserves, host copies, pinned budget, copy streams and OOM switches, built from the `compute` settings at every use (`core/inference/service.py: policy_from`), so a change reaches the next load without a restart","locale":"en"},"10876":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Units","locale":"en"},"10877":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/units.py`","locale":"en"},"10878":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Which parts of a module may stay off the device and which are fixed","locale":"en"},"10879":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`ModelHandle`","locale":"en"},"10880":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/handle.py`","locale":"en"},"10881":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"One component: whole or partly placed, host copies, hooks for streamed units","locale":"en"},"10882":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`Streamer`","locale":"en"},"10883":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/stream.py`","locale":"en"},"10884":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"One per device: the device copies of streamed units, their learnt order, prefetch on side streams, pinning","locale":"en"},"10885":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`ModelManager`","locale":"en"},"10886":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/manager.py`","locale":"en"},"10887":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Every registered component, the budget, eviction, partial loading, the OOM retry's clean-up, calibration, reporting","locale":"en"},"10888":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`RequestContext.use` / `run`","locale":"en"},"10889":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/context.py`","locale":"en"},"10890":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"A phase of a request; `run` adds the exact retry and the fallback","locale":"en"},"10891":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`peak_bytes`, `BufferCache`","locale":"en"},"10892":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/lm/cache.py`","locale":"en"},"10893":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"KV cache peaks for estimates; the static cache","locale":"en"},"10894":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`tiled_1d`","locale":"en"},"10895":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/nn/dsp/tiles.py`","locale":"en"},"10896":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Decoding in overlapping tiles (a fallback)","locale":"en"},"10897":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`DeviceService`","locale":"en"},"10898":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/runtime/device.py`","locale":"en"},"10899":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Free memory, `vram_limit_mb`, simulated devices","locale":"en"},"10900":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Training tools","locale":"en"},"10901":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"`mizuoto/training/memory.py`","locale":"en"},"10902":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#where-it-lives","content":"Checkpointing, presets, host optimizer, streamed frozen weights, estimates","locale":"en"},"10903":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"An engine with `PHASED = True` (every real engine Mizuoto ships; the dummy is not) wraps each part of a request in a phase, so only that part's components are placed, with that part's working memory set aside:","locale":"en"},"10904":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"**`ctx.use(handles, memory)`** is a context manager: the manager loads `handles` (whole or partly) with room for `memory` bytes of working memory and keeps them from giving up memory until the block ends. Use it for a phase that yields audio or consumes a generator.","locale":"en"},"10905":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"**`ctx.run(name, handles, fn, memory, generators=(), fallback=None)`** is the same around a pure function and adds the exact retry and the fallback (below). Use it wherever the block's only effect is its result.","locale":"en"},"10906":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"Components the phase does not name stay where they are and give up memory first when another phase needs it: an IndexTTS clone of a cached voice never places w2v-BERT or CAM++.","locale":"en"},"10907":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"An engine without `PHASED` gets every handle for the whole call, with `memory_required(task)` as the working memory.","locale":"en"},"10908":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#phases","content":"On the CPU the manager moves and counts nothing (the load and offload device are the same), so phases cost nothing there.","locale":"en"},"10909":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`ModelManager.load(handles, memory)` decides per device:","locale":"en"},"10910":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Need** = the handles' bytes not yet on the device + the working memory (the estimate times its calibration, below) + `compute.vram_reserve_mb`.","locale":"en"},"10911":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Free** = the driver's free memory plus what torch's caching allocator holds but does not use, capped by `compute.vram_limit_mb` (less what this process already holds) when that is set.","locale":"en"},"10912":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"If free memory falls short, other idle components give up memory (eviction, below), then free memory is measured again.","locale":"en"},"10913":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"If the handles fit, they load whole. Otherwise they load partly within the budget: free memory less working memory and reserve.","locale":"en"},"10914":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Mode (`compute.vram_mode`, `engines.<id>.vram_mode`)","locale":"en"},"10915":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Whole when it fits","locale":"en"},"10916":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"Otherwise","locale":"en"},"10917":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`auto` / `normal`","locale":"en"},"10918":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"10919":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"partly, down to the minimum","locale":"en"},"10920":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`high`","locale":"en"},"10921":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"10922":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"whole anyway, after idle components left whole; no partial loading","locale":"en"},"10923":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`low`","locale":"en"},"10924":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"only when the weights take at most half of the device's memory (`low_ratio`)","locale":"en"},"10925":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"partly, within that half","locale":"en"},"10926":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`none`","locale":"en"},"10927":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"never","locale":"en"},"10928":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"every unit streams: the fixed parts and the copies in flight only","locale":"en"},"10929":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"`shared` (automatic on MPS)","locale":"en"},"10930":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"yes","locale":"en"},"10931":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"whole: unified memory gains nothing from offloading, so no partial loading and no host copies","locale":"en"},"10932":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**No estimate refuses a load** (ComfyUI's way). In `high` the model is tried whole once idle components made room; in the other modes, below the minimum, it is tried at the minimum with a warning in the log. Only an allocation that really fails is an error. The catalogue's `vram_mb` is never checked before loading.","locale":"en"},"10933":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#the-budget","content":"**Two requests on one GPU.** With `compute.gpu_jobs` above 1, a request short of its minimum (in `high`: of its whole size) while another request holds components on the device waits for that request to let go, up to 10 minutes, checking for a cancel; then it tries.","locale":"en"},"10934":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A unit is a group of weights that moves together: a decoder layer, a DiT block, a vocoder stage (ComfyUI's dynamic-VRAM units).","locale":"en"},"10935":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A module may name its units with `memory_units() -> list[nn.Module]`. Otherwise each child of a `ModuleList` or `Sequential` of at least 1 MB is a unit (the outermost lists; a unit is never split further).","locale":"en"},"10936":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"**Only the weights of `nn.Linear`, `Conv1d/2d` and `ConvTranspose1d/2d` stream**, and of classes that declare `STREAMABLE = True` (IndexTTS's GPT-2 `Conv1D`). Their `forward` is where they are read.","locale":"en"},"10937":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"**Everything else is fixed**: on the device whenever the component is. That includes embeddings (codebooks are read through `.weight` as often as they are called), norms, scales, buffers, a parameter shared with a module outside the unit (tied embeddings and heads), the model's first parameter (code reads the model's device from it), units under 64 KB, modules marked `fixed` and everything under a module pinned to the CPU (`DeviceService.pin_to_cpu`, which never moves).","locale":"en"},"10938":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"An engine marks submodules on the handle with `handle.mark(module, mark)`:","locale":"en"},"10939":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Mark","locale":"en"},"10940":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Meaning","locale":"en"},"10941":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Used by","locale":"en"},"10942":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`fixed`","locale":"en"},"10943":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Never streams: code reads its weights outside its `forward`","locale":"en"},"10944":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"CosyVoice's HiFT F0 predictor; GPT-SoVITS's T2S (`memory_units() = []`)","locale":"en"},"10945":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`hot`","locale":"en"},"10946":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Runs many times per step: resident first","locale":"en"},"10947":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Qwen3-TTS's code predictor, VoxCPM's local DiT and encoder, Whisper's decoder","locale":"en"},"10948":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`cold`","locale":"en"},"10949":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"Runs once per request: streams first","locale":"en"},"10950":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A handle's `kind` decides which units stay resident when not all fit, after the `hot` ones:","locale":"en"},"10951":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`sequence` (an autoregressive LM, every layer once per token): units in execution order, so a fixed prefix of layers stays and the same layers stream every token;","locale":"en"},"10952":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"`oneshot` (encoders, decoders, flow steps over a whole input): the largest first, which keeps the copies in flight small.","locale":"en"},"10953":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#units-what-streams","content":"A handle's **minimum** is its fixed parts plus the copies of its largest units the streamer holds at once.","locale":"en"},"10954":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"A streamed unit's weights rest in RAM. Forward pre- and post-hooks on the unit, and on each module holding its weights, swap `param.data` to a device copy for the call and back after it. Hooks on the leaves make any path into a layer work (`layer.forward_step(…)`, `vae.decode`), and nothing in `ops` or in a layer's code changes. A component wholly on its device has no hooks at all, so the resident path costs nothing.","locale":"en"},"10955":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"The device's `Streamer`:","locale":"en"},"10956":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**keeps a few copies**: the running unit and those in flight (`compute.async_offload + 1`); a unit that runs again before its copy is dropped (CFG halves, beams) reuses it;","locale":"en"},"10957":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**learns the order** units run in (each unit's successor, as last seen) and, on CUDA and ROCm, **prefetches** the next units on side streams while the current one computes. The compute stream waits on a copy's event, and each copy is recorded on the compute stream so the caching allocator does not reuse it early. A unit entered out of order is copied there and then: correctness never depends on the order;","locale":"en"},"10958":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"**pins** the host copies of streamed weights (page-locked memory makes copies asynchronous and about twice as fast) within `compute.max_pinned_mb`, by default ComfyUI's budget: 40 % of RAM on Windows, elsewhere `max(0.4 × RAM, min(0.9 × RAM, RAM − 4 GB))`. Beyond it weights stay pageable and copy synchronously. XPU and MPS copy synchronously.","locale":"en"},"10959":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#weight-streaming","content":"Copies are made outside inference mode, in the weights' own dtype, so a streamed layer computes with the same kind of tensor as a resident one (an inference tensor takes other CPU kernels). Each fetch checks for a cancel. A `Compiled` module above a streamed unit runs eagerly (its graph would hold the weights), and CUDA graphs are captured only for models wholly on their device.","locale":"en"},"10960":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"With host copies (`compute.host_copies`: `auto` keeps them while free RAM stays above `compute.ram_reserve_mb`; `on`; `off`), a parameter placed on the device keeps its host tensor, so offloading it costs no transfer: the device is a cache over RAM. A partly loaded component always keeps them (its streamed units rest there). Buffers are always copied back (a module may update one in place).","locale":"en"},"10961":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"Unloading frees everything without copying anything back.","locale":"en"},"10962":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"A weight that changes after placement goes through the handle (`handle.replace(module)`: a voice's own GPT-SoVITS weights), so a host copy is never stale.","locale":"en"},"10963":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#host-copies-and-ram","content":"`compute.ram_reserve_mb` (empty: the larger of 2 GB and a tenth of the RAM): below it the runtime service drops the host copies of wholly loaded components first, then unloads idle models, least recently used first, never pinned or busy ones (`RuntimeService.relieve_ram`).","locale":"en"},"10964":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"When free memory falls short, idle components on the same device give up the shortfall, in this order:","locale":"en"},"10965":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"partly loaded ones (already slow; ComfyUI's first key);","locale":"en"},"10966":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"then those of models not in `engines.<id>.pinned_models` before pinned ones;","locale":"en"},"10967":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"then the least recently used.","locale":"en"},"10968":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Components in use by any request are never victims; the running model's components that the current phase does not need are. Each victim gives up units, in reverse fill order, until the shortfall is met (`partially_unload`); in `high` and `shared` it leaves whole. `empty_cache` runs after an eviction, never per request.","locale":"en"},"10969":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Partly loading a phase's components: the smaller ones load whole first, each leaving the others at least their minimum, and the largest gets what is left, its remaining units streaming.","locale":"en"},"10970":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#eviction","content":"Above the manager, `RuntimeService` keeps at most `compute.max_resident_models` models loaded, unloads idle ones after `compute.unload_after_minutes` (server only), moves every idle model off a GPU before a fine-tune there (`release_device`), and after a job fails for running out of memory unloads every model not in use from every GPU (`ComputeService.release_after_oom`, ComfyUI's `unload_all_models`). See Runtime.","locale":"en"},"10971":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"**The exact retry** (`compute.oom_retry`, on). When a phase run through `ctx.run` runs out of device memory:","locale":"en"},"10972":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the attempt's exception frame is left first (it holds the failed attempt's tensors);","locale":"en"},"10973":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"`manager.after_oom`: every other idle component on the device leaves it, the streamer's cached copies go, and each of the phase's components is capped at half of what it had on the device (`ModelHandle.cap_bytes`, kept until it is offloaded, so the next requests do not run out too);","locale":"en"},"10974":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the request's generators, its `rng` and any the engine names (`generators=`) are put back as they were before the first attempt;","locale":"en"},"10975":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the phase runs again: the same draws, the same result, slower. At most two retries; the output records `applied.oom_retries` and a warning.","locale":"en"},"10976":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"A phase under `ctx.use` (one that yields audio or consumes a generator) is not retried, so its estimate must bound what it can reach.","locale":"en"},"10977":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"**Fallbacks** (`compute.oom_fallback`, off). After the retries an engine's cheaper way runs, if it gave one (`ctx.run(…, fallback=(what, fn))`). Its output differs slightly, so it is recorded in `applied.fallbacks` with a warning:","locale":"en"},"10978":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Engine","locale":"en"},"10979":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Fallback","locale":"en"},"10980":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"ASR","locale":"en"},"10981":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"one 30 s window at a time","locale":"en"},"10982":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"GPT-SoVITS","locale":"en"},"10983":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"one text at a time","locale":"en"},"10984":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"F5-TTS","locale":"en"},"10985":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"Vocos in tiles","locale":"en"},"10986":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"IndexTTS","locale":"en"},"10987":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"BigVGAN in tiles","locale":"en"},"10988":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-136","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"10989":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-137","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"the VAE in tiles","locale":"en"},"10990":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-138","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#running-out-of-memory","content":"`tiled_1d` decodes 256 frames at a time with 32 frames of overlap, cross-faded (ComfyUI's audio VAE tiling). Qwen3-TTS's codec already decodes in chunks; CosyVoice's HiFT carries a phase that tiles would break.","locale":"en"},"10991":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-139","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"A phase's estimate decides whether its weights fit whole, so it is sized by the request (its text tokens, batch rows, the reference's length), not by the worst case `max_new_tokens` allows. Setting aside memory the request never uses pushes weights out for nothing: a worst case of 8192 frames, doubled, was 2.4 GB for a Qwen3-TTS sentence that uses about 20 MB.","locale":"en"},"10992":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-140","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"Language models' caches are sized by the text: Qwen3-TTS 16 frames a text token, IndexTTS 24 codes, GPT-SoVITS 16 a phone, CosyVoice upstream's 20 a token, each within `max_new_tokens`; Fish Speech's batches by their true bound; in the dtype and KV width the model computes in.","locale":"en"},"10993":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-141","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"`runtime.lm.cache.peak_bytes(position_bytes, layers, positions, kind, rows)` gives a growing cache's peak: for `dynamic`, every layer plus one layer's new copy while a step concatenates (each layer's old tensors are freed before the next), not twice the cache; for `static`, the doubled buffers plus one old buffer while it grows.","locale":"en"},"10994":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-142","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"A rare generation that outruns its estimate under `ctx.run` runs again with more weights streamed, exactly.","locale":"en"},"10995":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-143","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#working-memory-estimates","content":"**Calibration.** After each phase on CUDA or XPU, the manager measures its working memory: the peak above the placed weights, less the streamer's copies at their most and less what the phase keeps afterwards (CUDA graphs' pools, buffers kept with the model). When that exceeds the estimate, it remembers the ratio (times 1.1, at most 16) per phase, keyed by the engine and its components' names (`qwen3tts:talker`), and sets aside that much more next time. The ratios are kept in `<cache>/memory/calibration.json` (format version 2; a file of another version is ignored).","locale":"en"},"10996":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-144","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"Every output's `meta.memory`: `streamed_mb` (copied from RAM during the request), `loaded_mb`, `size_mb`, and for partly loaded components `partial`: per component the `reason` (the budget fell short, `vram_mode low` or `none`, capped after running out of memory, or gave up memory for another), the `mode`, `free_mb`, `working_mb` and `reserve_mb` weighed, `loaded_mb`, `size_mb` and `gave_up_for`. The log says the same when it happens.","locale":"en"},"10997":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-145","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"`GET /api/v1/runtime` and `mizuoto runtime`: per model `loaded_bytes`, `streamed_bytes` and `host_bytes`; the web UI's loaded-models sheet draws them as a bar.","locale":"en"},"10998":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-146","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#reporting","content":"`mizuoto doctor` has a `memory` finding with the policy in effect (mode, reserve, limit, RAM reserve, host copies, pinned budget, streams, retry and fallbacks).","locale":"en"},"10999":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-147","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"`compute.kv_cache = static` gives the Qwen-family caches (Qwen3-TTS, CosyVoice 2/3) preallocated buffers that double when full (`BufferCache`): no per-step concatenation, no transient copy, less fragmentation. Attention reads views with the buffer's strides, which some kernels sum in another order. Identical on the CPU; opt-in until it is checked identical on a GPU.","locale":"en"},"11000":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-148","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#static-kv-cache-and-the-cuda-allocator","content":"`compute.cuda_allocator` (`native`, `async`: cudaMallocAsync, `expandable`: expandable segments; `auto` is `native`) is set by the host in `PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF` before CUDA is first used, never by engine code, and handed to training stages. A value the environment already sets wins; a change applies at the next start.","locale":"en"},"11001":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-149","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"`DeviceService(simulate={\"cpu\": bytes}, strict=True)` (the setting `compute.simulate_vram_mb`, for tests and the parity harness) makes the CPU play a GPU of that size. The manager places weights by copying them, streams units, and counts what it placed against the simulated size. In strict mode every weight off the device rests as a meta tensor, so:","locale":"en"},"11002":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-150","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"a component used outside its phase fails (it was not placed);","locale":"en"},"11003":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-151","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"a unit's weight read outside its `forward` fails (it is not there).","locale":"en"},"11004":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-152","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#simulated-devices-and-tests","content":"`tests/engines_native/streaming.py` runs each tiny engine so, with every unit streamed (mode `none`), with half the room between its minimum and its size, and whole, and compares each output with the plain CPU run: identical. The parity harness does the same on real models (`--setting compute.simulate_vram_mb=…`). See Testing.","locale":"en"},"11005":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-153","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Training stages run in their own process and own their device: before a GPU stage the host moves every idle model off that GPU, and the train job holds every slot of `gpu:<dev>` (`JobSpec.exclusive`). Inside the stage, `training.memory` chooses a preset of `mizuoto/training/memory.py`'s tools; a method's own hyperparameters win:","locale":"en"},"11006":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-154","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Preset","locale":"en"},"11007":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-155","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Adds","locale":"en"},"11008":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-156","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Results","locale":"en"},"11009":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-157","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`standard` (default)","locale":"en"},"11010":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-158","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"nothing: each recipe exactly","locale":"en"},"11011":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-159","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"identical to upstream","locale":"en"},"11012":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-160","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`low`","locale":"en"},"11013":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-161","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"gradient checkpointing over each block (`gradient_checkpointing`: `blocks`, `every:k`; VoxCPM, Qwen3-TTS, OmniVoice, F5-TTS, CosyVoice), the recomputation with the global generators *and the trainer's own* put back; F5-TTS's EMA copy in RAM; VoxCPM's LoRA frozen weights in the autocast dtype","locale":"en"},"11014":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-162","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"identical (tested per trainer on the CPU)","locale":"en"},"11015":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-163","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"`lowest`","locale":"en"},"11016":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-164","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"also AdamW's state in RAM, stepped on the CPU (`host_optimizer`; Qwen3-TTS, OmniVoice, F5-TTS without a gradient scaler); VoxCPM's LoRA base weights streamed from RAM for each product (`streamed_linear`)","locale":"en"},"11017":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-165","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"the optimizer's sums may differ in the last bits on a GPU","locale":"en"},"11018":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-166","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#training","content":"Always: a GPU stage reports `memory/peak_mb`; `MethodInfo.memory_mb` estimates a run's peak per preset from the model's size, and a GPU run warns in its log, naming the preset that would fit, when the estimate exceeds the free memory. Trainers themselves are in Trainers.","locale":"en"},"11019":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-167","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Wrap every module with weights in a `ModelHandle`** and list it in `self.handles`. Give an autoregressive LM `kind=\"sequence\"`; mark parts that run many times per step `hot`, and modules whose weights code reads outside their `forward` `fixed`. Name units with `memory_units()` when the default split (children of `ModuleList`/`Sequential`) does not fit the model.","locale":"en"},"11020":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-168","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Never move modules yourself.** Build on the host (or on `spec.device`; the handle takes it from there), and let the manager place them. A float64 module on a device without float64 uses `float64_device` and `pin_to_cpu`. Swap weights with `handle.replace`.","locale":"en"},"11021":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-169","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Set `PHASED = True` and run each part in a phase**: `ctx.run` for pure blocks, `ctx.use` for blocks that yield or consume a generator. Pass `generators=` for any generator the engine owns besides the context's, so a retry replays it.","locale":"en"},"11022":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-170","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Estimate each phase's working memory from the request**: KV caches with `peak_bytes` in the dtype the model computes in, attention scores, CFG batches, decoder activations by length. Under `ctx.use`, bound what the phase can reach.","locale":"en"},"11023":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-171","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Offer a fallback** where a cheaper exact-enough way exists (tiles, one item at a time), with a short description of what it does.","locale":"en"},"11024":{"id":"/en/docs/development-memory-172","page_id":"/en/docs/development-memory","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-memory#writing-an-engine-for-it","content":"**Run the tiny model under `streaming.py`**: every unit streamed, half, none, strict. A failure there names the component used outside its phase or the weight read outside its unit.","locale":"en"},"11025":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"page","content":"Performance record","breadcrumbs":["Mizuoto Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance","locale":"en"},"11026":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance","content":"How Mizuoto's speed is measured under the rule that results never change (perf.py, train_perf.py), the conditions of each measurement, and what each round of work changed: the CPU rounds, the attention dispatcher, CUDA graphs per engine and torch.compile.","locale":"en"},"11027":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"How it is measured","locale":"en"},"11028":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"Conditions","locale":"en"},"11029":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Where the time goes on the CPU","locale":"en"},"11030":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-1-cpu-settings-and-exact-clean-ups","content":"CPU, round 1: CPU settings and exact clean-ups","locale":"en"},"11031":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cpu-round-2-training","content":"CPU, round 2: training","locale":"en"},"11032":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#the-attention-dispatcher","content":"The attention dispatcher","locale":"en"},"11033":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpu-decoding-without-waits-as-cuda-graphs","content":"GPU: decoding without waits, as CUDA graphs","locale":"en"},"11034":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#qwen3-tts","content":"Qwen3-TTS","locale":"en"},"11035":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#fish-speech","content":"Fish Speech","locale":"en"},"11036":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#cosyvoice-23","content":"CosyVoice 2/3","locale":"en"},"11037":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#voxcpm","content":"VoxCPM","locale":"en"},"11038":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#gpt-sovits","content":"GPT-SoVITS","locale":"en"},"11039":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#indextts","content":"IndexTTS","locale":"en"},"11040":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#on-the-cpu","content":"On the CPU","locale":"en"},"11041":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#torchcompile-on-the-cpu","content":"`torch.compile` on the CPU","locale":"en"},"11042":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-performance#not-done-because-it-changes-results","content":"Not done because it changes results","locale":"en"},"11043":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance","content":"Speed work on the inference and training code follows one rule: **the results do not change**. Every optimisation recorded here gives the same audio sample for sample, and the same losses and saved weights bit for bit, as the code before it on the CPU, where the engines' parity with upstream is established. On a GPU, \"the same\" means identical to the code before on the same GPU, driver, torch and settings. Faster paths that change the arithmetic are opt-in features, described in Acceleration; the user-facing settings are in Performance.","locale":"en"},"11044":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Tool","locale":"en"},"11045":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"What it does","locale":"en"},"11046":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`scripts/parity/perf.py run <cases.jsonl>… --name NAME [--only ID] [--repeat N] [--device D] [--setting k=v] [--reference NAME] [--profile] [--torch-profile]`","locale":"en"},"11047":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Runs parity cases through the whole stack in one process, each `--repeat` times (3): the first loads the model and prepares the voice, the others are warm. Records the wall time, load, first chunk, engine total, real-time factor, and the time inside each phase (`ctx.use` / `ctx.run`, exclusive of nested phases, plus the time outside phases). Compares the audio sample for sample across the runs and with `--reference`: an earlier run of that name, else the case's parity fixture","locale":"en"},"11048":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`perf.py compare --a NAME --b NAME [--report out.md]`, `perf.py show NAME`","locale":"en"},"11049":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"The warm runs of two rounds side by side, the speed-up and the phases that changed most","locale":"en"},"11050":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`scripts/parity/train_perf.py run <engine> --fixture F --name NAME [--reference NAME] [--profile]`, `compare`","locale":"en"},"11051":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"A fine-tune with a parity fixture's recipe through the whole stack, every stage a child process as in use: each stage's time, the time per step, every metric and the SHA-256 of every weight file the export wrote, compared with an earlier run","locale":"en"},"11052":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`--profile` adds an uncounted run under cProfile (in-process stages for `train_perf.py`); `--torch-profile` lists the operators by their own time.","locale":"en"},"11053":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Records go to `MIZUOTO_PERF_DIR` (default `~/code_workspace/experiment/perf`), one JSON file per case and round.","locale":"en"},"11054":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"A warm time is the fastest warm run: the machine's other work only ever slows a run down. Comparisons that matter are interleaved, the two codes in separate processes, one after the other.","locale":"en"},"11055":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Freeze the code being measured (a worktree at the commit) and run the baseline: `perf.py run` over the case set, `train_perf.py run` for each trainer.","locale":"en"},"11056":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Profile (`--profile`, `--torch-profile`) to find where the time goes; for a kernel-level change, write a micro-benchmark and check the operation's output with `torch.equal` over the shapes the engines use.","locale":"en"},"11057":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"Run the changed code with `--reference` set to the baseline: every case must give identical audio, every fine-tune identical metrics and weight hashes. Then run the parity stack for the engines whose code changed, and the test suite.","locale":"en"},"11058":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"`perf.py compare` for the table; record the round here.","locale":"en"},"11059":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#how-it-is-measured","content":"The inference set is thirteen cases: Qwen3-TTS `base-clone-en` and `cv-en-aiden`, F5-TTS `clone-en`, OmniVoice `clone-en`, CosyVoice `v2-clone-en` and `v3-clone-en`, GPT-SoVITS `v2-clone-zh` and `v4-clone-zh`, Fish Speech `clone-en`, IndexTTS `v2-clone-en` and `v15-clone-en`, VoxCPM `v2-clone-en` and `v15-clone-en`. The training set is one fine-tune per trainer on an 11-clip dataset.","locale":"en"},"11060":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"**CPU**: AMD Ryzen 9 5900X (12 cores; torch uses 12 threads), 125 GB RAM, torch 2.8.0 with MKL 2024.2 and oneDNN 3.7.1, Python 3.11; fp32, except VoxCPM in bf16 (its upstream default). Other work on the machine takes about 9 % of its threads; a run whose compute thread is preempted stalls, so single runs vary by up to ±10 %.","locale":"en"},"11061":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#conditions","content":"**GPU**: two RTX 3090 (24 GB), **shared with another workload** that held about 21 GB of each and kept both 100 % busy. Every GPU number below is inflated by that sharing; comparisons were interleaved on the same GPU and torch. Models that did not fit beside the other workload were measured as their decoding loop alone: the real sizes, random weights and a few of their layers, which is labelled where it applies.","locale":"en"},"11062":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"The warm request before the CPU rounds:","locale":"en"},"11063":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Engine, case","locale":"en"},"11064":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Audio s","locale":"en"},"11065":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Warm s","locale":"en"},"11066":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Phases (s)","locale":"en"},"11067":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Qwen3-TTS base-clone-en","locale":"en"},"11068":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"5.44","locale":"en"},"11069":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"34.25","locale":"en"},"11070":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"talker 30.2, codec decoder 4.0","locale":"en"},"11071":{"id":"/en/docs/development-performance-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-performance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-performance#where-the-time-goes-on-the-cpu","content":"Qwen3-TTS cv-en-aiden","locale":